## Legal caveats (not legal advice; have counsel review before any public release)
- **The change only applies going forward.** Copies already released under GPL-3.0, or published while `package.json` said ISC, stay licensed that way to the people who received them.
- **Sole authorship must really hold.** Before release, confirm there is no vendored, copied, generated or AI-assisted code from other sources in the tree. Future contributions will also need a policy, such as a CLA or a stated "GPL-2.0-only with this exception" rule.
- **This is not literally the Linux kernel's licence.** The kernel is GPL-2.0-only plus a narrow note saying user programs are not derived works; it has no linking exception for libraries. This draft is closer to the Classpath style. The strength of the copyleft on datom.world's own code is the same as the kernel's.
- **Each module's own licence still applies.** The exception removes the GPL's objection only. EPL-1.0 source and choice-of-law terms, and Apache-2.0 NOTICE and patent terms, still bind those modules; clause (b) says so.
- **EPL-2.0 is listed directly** so the draft doesn't depend on whether each EPL-2.0 library names GPL as an allowed "Secondary License".
- **Datomic peer:** check the exact version used. Datomic Pro binaries have been Apache-2.0 since 2023, but older versions were proprietary and are not covered.
- **Clojure and JVM linking:** whether loading code at runtime creates a "work based on" datom.world is legally unsettled. The draft is meant to work either way.
- **Other dependency licences were not audited.** That includes MIT, BSD, LGPL and GPL-3.0-only libraries. A GPL-3.0-only dependency would be incompatible with GPL-2.0-only.
- **No conflict with GPL-2.0 section 6 ("no further restrictions"):** the exception only adds permissions.

